<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Llotja de la Seda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1482">1482</a>–<a href="/years/1533">1533</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Silk Exchange of Valencia, Lonja de la Seda, Valencia Silk Exchange</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Llotja de la Seda, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silk</a> Exchange, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> is a late Gothic merchants' exchange whose great hall of twisted columns was built in 1482–1498 under the master Pere Compte, with later wings completed by 1533. It housed the trade in silk and other goods when Valencia was the richest city of the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>. It has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage site since 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/llotja-de-la-seda.jpg" alt="Llotja de la Seda" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felivet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Llonja.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Behind the present building stood an earlier one of the fourteenth century, called the Oil Exchange (Llotja de l'Oli in Valencian, Lonja del Aceite in Spanish), which was used for trade, including agricultural oils. In 1348 percale, a type of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, was traded there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>'s commercial prosperity reached its peak in the fifteenth century and led to the construction of a new building. Its design was derived from a similar structure, the Lonja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>, built by the architect Guillem Sagrera in 1448.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Llotja is a late Valencian Gothic civil building made up of three parts, together with the Orange Garden, a walled courtyard. The main hall, the Sala de Contractació or Trading Hall, is a large and richly decorated space supported by twisted columns; it was the financial centre where merchants worked out contracts. The architect in charge, Pere Compte, built it in only fifteen years.</p><p>The side wing is the Pavilion of the Consulate, seat of the Consolat del Mar, the first maritime merchant tribunal formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Its first two floors held the main rooms, and the second floor had a richly decorated ceiling; the rooms still have their original furnishings. The third part is the central tower, in which the tribunal occasionally imprisoned merchants for debt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>A blue band runs along all four walls of the Trading Hall and carries an inscription in golden letters. It begins by saying that the house was built in fifteen years, and it praises honest trade, which does not deceive with the tongue and does not lend money at interest. The Valencian scholar Joan Francesc Mira read it as showing that one did not need to be a Protestant or a foreigner to enter into good trade, and as an example of ethical principles joined to the economy.</p><p>Further building and decoration continued after the hall was finished, among them the Consolat del Mar, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> building attached to the Llotja. In the following centuries it served as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> exchange. In 1996 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listed it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, describing it as a wholly exceptional example of a secular building in late Gothic style that illustrates the power and wealth of one of the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> mercantile cities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lonja de la Seda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Black Death of 1348 hit Catalonia especially hard, and in the fifteenth century Valencia overtook Barcelona as the largest and richest city of the Crown, building its Silk Exchange, the <b>Llotja de la Seda</b>, in 1482–1498.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
